 ## Real GPU acceptance and resource benchmarks
 
 The new opt-in `DanceFlowShaderMetrics` capture adds per-frame CPU/GPU/GC/video-memory observations, type-7 p50/p95/p99 summaries, and explicit *unavailable* counters. For reproducible same-device A/B/C/D/M benchmark runs, generated URP scenes, Unity Player build scripts, independent Python analysis, and a real GPU readback test, use **[Phase 2 benchmarking and acceptance](docs/05-benchmarking.md)**. Benchmark baseline/effect shaders now use matching URP Unlit passes and a non-overlapping object grid. These are tools, not claims of verified GPU performance; actual Unity Editor/device runs and a licensed hardware runner remain necessary.
+
+
+## Phase 3 — native recordings (first slice; 0.4.0 not released)
+
+Canonical `dancerudiments.recording` schema 1 files are now parsed with a strict
+64 MiB JSON limit, duplicate-key rejection, exact clock/metadata validation,
+upstream float64 twelve-decimal normalisation, arbitrary named channels, and
+non-looping signed native 64-pip boundaries. `RecordingBinaryCodec` preserves
+row-major float64 values in an independently hash-verified DFR1 binary cache.
+
+In Unity choose **Assets > DanceFlow > Import DanceRudiments Recording JSON...**
+to create a metadata-only `MotionRecordingAsset` and a separate `.bytes` payload
+under `Assets/DanceFlowRecordings/`, with unique non-overwriting paths.
+`MotionTransformDriver` maps up to three named channels to local XYZ offsets;
+association with the master source timeline or explicit beat retiming must be
+acknowledged. These components consume the existing Phase 1 clock state, not an
+independent delta-time clock. Multiple active drivers share one decoded cache.
+
+```csharp
+using DanceFlow.Unity.Motion;
+MotionRecording data = asset.OpenVerified();
+int bass = data.Descriptor.ChannelIndex("bass");
+double value = data.SampleExact(data.Clock.StartPip, bass);
+```
+
+Unity-independent managed checks: `dotnet run --project tools~/recording-checks/RecordingChecks.csproj -c Release`.
+Independent fixture evidence: `python tools~/recording-checks/verify_fixture.py`.
+See [Phase 3 recording implementation and known gaps](docs/04-recording-format.md).
+GPU recording buffers, further property bindings, optional chunk streaming and
+licensed Unity performance tests remain future Phase 3 work. Package version
+remains `0.3.0` until acceptance, and no real GPU performance is claimed.
+
+## Phase 3 — recording, song-reactive samples and GPU motion windows
+
+The optional **Song Reactive Landscape** package sample generates animated trees,
+batched grass, rolling terrain, glowing stars, fog and geometry-warp effects.
+`MotionSignalBus` publishes four user-selected recording channels to global
+shaders; `MotionPropertyDriver` maps a channel to typed Unity light/material/
+particle targets; a bounded opt-in full-recording `GraphicsBuffer` is available
+on supported GPUs. **Synthetic preview is clearly labelled and never asserted
+to be matched song data.**
+
+Use Package Manager > DanceFlow for Unity > Samples > **Song Reactive Landscape**,
+then **Tools > DanceFlow > Samples** to create a synthetic, Arcadians, The Truth
+is a Virus, or Star Lights Glow demonstration scene. Actual song/audio recordings
+are not bundled, and Star Lights Glow BPM/beat phase are not guessed. Import
+corresponding DanceRudiments/StemLab recordings to enable genuine song motion.
+
+See [showcase guide](docs/09-song-reactive-showcase.md) and the sample's README.
+Runtime shader/Unity compilation and real GPU demonstration remain unverified.
+
+### Bind real song assets (new)
+
+The optional **Song Reactive Landscape** sample now includes **Tools > DanceFlow >
+Samples > Create Source-Verified Song Profile...**. Import the exact original
+master into your project (prefer the original WAV for precise source frames),
+import a `dancerudiments.recording` using **Assets > DanceFlow**, and optionally
+attach the complete `stemlab.beat-timeline.v1` from the same source. The wizard
+hashes the actual master file and validates the timing map; per-song audio and
+recording data stay under **your** `Assets/`, never inside this UPM package.
+
+Select the generated SongShowcaseProfile asset and choose **Tools > DanceFlow >
+Samples > Create Scene From Selected Song Profile**. Playback keeps the actual
+song clock authoritative. Real recording playback requires a user-approved
+source-clock correspondence or an explicit musical retime. This cannot establish
+beat phase by guessing from a BPM hint. Star Lights Glow's *Mystery Liquid Kick*
+and *Anthem* remain independent profiles with no invented BPM.
+
+A standalone receipt tool can preflight local StemLab `.json` / `.json.gz`
+recordings and audio hashes before Unity import:
+
+```
+python tools~/showcase-checks/prepare_song_assets.py --song arcadians \
+  --master "../stemlab/examples/arcadians/Arcadians - 320kbps.mp3" \
+  --recording "./my-analysis/motion.json.gz" --bpm 145 \
+  --output "./local-assets/arcadians-receipt.json"
+```
+
+GPU recording shaders now support long captures using a **4 MiB maximum sliding
+window with one row of overlap**, not an unbounded upload or hidden truncation.
+The shader cursor uses the exact same signed source pip calculated by the CPU.
+GPU/Unity integration still requires running the licensed Unity test workflows;
+no claim of real hardware acceptance is made by standalone contract checks.
